        }
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "If you connect an image to an NKD Reference node, the Popup Preview window can use it to compare it with the result."
      ],
      "color": "#223",
      "bgcolor": "#335"
    },
    {
      "id": 1370,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1085,
        1660
      ],
      "size": [
        580,
        805
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 10,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "ℹ️ Node Info",
      "properties": {
        "ue_properties": {
          "widget_ue_connectable": {},
          "version": "7.8",
          "input_ue_unconnectable": {}
        }
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "# NKD Klein Tools\n\nThese two nodes (**NKD Klein Presampling** and **NKD Klein Postsampling**) work in pairs to deal with all the Flux Klein internal logic, prompt encoding, reference setup, mask prep, optional detail crop, and the final composite onto your original image in case you do an inpainting.\n\n## The two ways to inpaint\n### Classic inpainting (local img2img for refinement)\n\nIf you want to use Klein as your generative model, make sure to enable the _Bypass Reference_ option. This lets you do a classic image-to-image pass to refine underlying details.\n\nUse this when the masked region is *almost right* and you just want to clean it up. Soft textures, small artifacts. Stuff like that.\n\nKlein behaves like a normal img2img model in this mode. The latent under the mask seeds the result, the prompt nudges it. Above 0.75 it starts making things up. Below 0.20 nothing happens. The 0.40 to 0.60 range tightens detail without changing the composition.\n\n### Inpaint in editing mode\n\nThe great thing about Klein is that you can use it as an editing model during inpainting. To do it, make sure you have _Bypass Reference_ disabled, so the model can treat your input as a reference. This is super useful for swapping out specific details in your masked areas, like changing a jacket's color or someone's hairstyle.\n\nIn this mode the prompt works more like an instruction than a description, like:\n\n- *\"make the jacket red\"*\n- *\"turn the mug into a glass\"*\n- *\"add aviator sunglasses\"*\n \n\nKlein looks at the unmasked area as context and tries to match style, lighting and perspective when filling the mask, so the edit blends into the rest of the image instead of looking pasted in. You need to keep the denoise high here (0.85–1.0 is fine) because you actually want the masked region rebuilt from scratch.\n\nThe reference is what holds everything together, not the underlying latent. Great for surgical edits where img2img would either change too little or break the surrounding image.\n\n## Inpaint Blend\n\nControls how the edit meets the original at the mask edge.\n\n- With **high values** the edit blends into its surroundings throught the inpainting (not compositing). It benefits a lot from a blured mask and it's especially helpful with the \"classic inpainting mode\". \n\n- **Lower values** push the transition toward a hard, literal mask boundary. This is good if you are feeding a precise mask (from a 3D ID pass or photoshop). It can also help the model follow your prompts more closely in \"editing mode\", as it can look more accurately at the context around the mask.\n\n## Detailing\n\nCrops and zooms into the masked region before sampling. The model spends its full resolution on just that area. Great for faces, hands, eyes, small text.\n\n**Padding** controls how much room around the mask gets included. More padding gives the model more context. Less padding keeps the focus tight.\n\n\n\n## Quick rule of thumb:\n\n- **Refining what's already there** → Bypass Reference **on**, denoise around 0.40 - 0.60.\n- **Replacing or editing what's there** → Bypass Reference **off**, denoise 1, and prompt like giving orders: \"_remove whatever_\" \"_turn whatever into this thing_\"."
      ],
      "color": "#223",
      "bgcolor": "#335"
    },
